      "confidence": 0.0,
      "narrative": "This is a literature screening alert referencing a published case series. The alert provides only bibliographic information (title, authors, journal, DOI) and indicates that the full text is attached. No individual patient data, adverse events, or clinical details are presented in the alert itself. The source is a metadata notification, not a case report.",
      "fields": [
        {
          "field_group": "NARRATIVE",
          "field_path": "narrative",
          "value": "A 34-year-old man was prescribed Domitrelle 2 mg twice daily for generalised anxiety disorder commencing 2 February 2026. On 29 March 2026, eight weeks into treatment, he developed agranulocytosis with a neutrophil count of 0.3 x 10^9/L, fever and mouth ulceration. The event was life-threatening. Domitrelle was withdrawn and he was managed with granulocyte colony-stimulating factor; the neutrophil count was recovering at the time of writing. A 45-year-old male was prescribed Pralextin 100 micrograms/dose two puffs as required for asthma maintenance commencing 5 May 2026, and was additionally given Astelvia 1 g three times daily on 18 May for post-operative prophylaxis. On 21 May 2026 he developed angioedema with swelling of the lips and tongue and difficulty swallowing, which was life-threatening. On the following day he developed persistent dizziness. He attended the emergency department and was treated with intramuscular adrenaline. Both medicines were stopped and he recovered.",
          "typed_value": null,
